  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana to check how it renders. I also haven't confirmed the rule has run or what it returns.\n\n**Data**\n- The Kibana Sample Data Logs view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`) holds web access logs with a `timestamp` field. The main fields are `response` (HTTP status), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `geo.src`/`geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `referer`, `request`/`url`, `host` and `agent`.\n- `response` is a text field, so I queried it through its `response.keyword` subfield.\n\n**Dashboard** \"EFF-800f855d Web logs overview\" (ID `44d48728-0b38-4e69-a44d-e6abedcf5871`, default time range last 7 days) has four panels:\n- Total requests (count).\n- Average bytes.\n- Unique clients (unique count of `clientip`).\n- A line chart of requests over time.\n\n**Rule** \"EFF-800f855d 5xx spike\" (ID `056b3040-00f0-456e-9b27-e2c0cb917641`):\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute.\n- Each run counts documents in the last 5 minutes where `response.keyword` matches `5xx`.\n- It fires when that count is greater than 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so a firing only shows up as an alert in Kibana and sends no notification.",
